\`cloudflare.Worker(name, fn, options)\` in \`@tomlarkworthy/cloudflare-iac\` makes the service as a value in notebook code: an object that can run the function in the tab, report its state, and deploy it. \`service.deploy()\` is a call to the guard (\`infra.apply\`) that puts the function on a Worker with the cells it depends on. Tom, 2026-10-05: "service = cloudflare.Worker(…) to create the abstraction in notebook code, then service.deploy(…) to make an RPC call to deploy it".

\`\`\`js
import {cloudflare, secrets, inbox} from "@tomlarkworthy/cloudflare-iac"

verify = async (body, header, key) => { … }             // an ordinary function cell

hook = cloudflare.Worker("whatsapp", async (request, { caller }) => {
  const body = await request.arrayBuffer();
  if (!await verify(body, request.headers.get("x-hub-signature-256"), await secrets.WHATSAPP_APP_SECRET))
    return new Response("bad signature", { status: 401 });
  await inbox.append({ source: "whatsapp", key: id(body), body: text(body) });
  return new Response("ok");
}, { paths: [{ path: "/hooks/whatsapp", who: "anyone" }], methods: { "com.lopecode.brain.whatsapp.send": sendLexicon } })

hook.fetch(new Request(…))     // runs the function in the tab
hook.status                    // in sync, changed, not installed, …
hook.deploy()                  // asks the guard to deploy this one Worker
\`\`\`

**Chasing ancestors.** The cell that declares the Worker is a runtime variable with inputs, and each input is a variable with inputs. The emit walks that graph upward, through imports into other modules, and writes each cell it reaches as one line of a plain ES module in dependency order:

\`\`\`js
const verify = (function _verify(){return( async (body, header, key) => { … } )})();
const hook_fn = (function(verify, secrets, inbox){return( async (request, { caller }) => { … } )})(verify, secrets, inbox);
\`\`\`

\`@tomlarkworthy/distiller\` does this for a whole notebook ("pure ES6 modules. That means no runtime", read 2026-10-04). Its open issues are imports and standard library calls; this emit must follow imports.

| a chased cell may be | refused, naming the cell |
|---|---|
| a function, a constant, an object, a promise of one | a generator; a \`viewof\` or \`mutable\` cell; a cell depending on \`html\`, \`md\`, \`width\`, \`invalidation\`, \`now\` or another DOM or reactive builtin; a computed secret name |

**Library cells.** A cell whose value is an ES module loaded from a file attachment is carried as a file: the attachment becomes a second module part and the emitted line is \`import * as hono from "./lib/hono.js"\`. It must be one self-contained module that does not call \`eval\`.

**Platform cells.** Some cells of \`cloudflare-iac\` stand for a Cloudflare resource. The walk stops at them. Each works in the notebook; on the Worker the wrapper supplies the real one, and the ones reached decide the Worker's bindings.

| cell | in the notebook | on the Worker | binding it causes |
|---|---|---|---|
| \`secrets\` | \`await secrets.NAME\` loads the value for the signed-in owner (\`secret.get\`) | reads the secret | the names found as \`secrets.NAME\` in the chased cells |
| \`rows\` | a key-value table kept by the core for that Worker name | the Worker's own SQLite | a Durable Object with SQLite, used for storage only |
| \`inbox\` | \`inbox.append\`, \`inbox.list\`, \`inbox.done\` over HTTP | \`inbox.append\` over the core binding | the core |
| \`blobs\` | get and put by SHA-256 over HTTP | R2 | the bucket |
| \`xrpc\` | calls any method over HTTP with the owner's cookie | calls it over the core binding | the core |
| \`fetch\` | the tab's fetch, through the proxy recipe when CORS needs it | the Worker's fetch | none |

**What \`cloudflare.Worker\` returns.** An object for the service. \`fetch(request)\` runs the function in the tab against the notebook side of the platform cells. \`status\` is its hash state. \`deploy()\` saves the notebook if needed and calls \`infra.apply\` for this one Worker; the guard's checks, and approval if it is on, apply as for any deploy. Declaring uploads nothing. Apply in the Services list is \`deploy()\` for every row that differs, sent as one batch.

**The wrapper** is the fixed part of every upload. It supplies the platform cells, calls the function or \`app.fetch\`, answers \`/xrpc/_health\` and \`getInfo\`, records the last 100 requests, and turns a throw into an XRPC error.

### Testing in the tab

- **Call it.** \`hook.fetch(request)\` runs the function with the owner's real secrets and the core's real rows and inbox.
- **Simulate it.** \`simulate(hook, { secrets, fetch })\` imports the emitted module text from a blob URL with platform cells replaced, and exposes \`sim.inbox\`, \`sim.rows\` and \`sim.requests\`. With no \`fetch\` fake an outbound call throws. A cell the emit wrote out wrongly fails here as it would on Cloudflare.
- A recipe's \`test_*\` cells use \`simulate\` with recorded requests as fixtures.
- **Hono** is the router (Tom, 2026-10-05). The core and any recipe that wants routing are Hono apps; Hono reaches a Worker as a library cell. Hono 4.13.12 as one 18.8 KB module answered six requests in headless Chromium on a \`file://\` page on 2026-10-05 (Appendix B).

Neither way shows Cloudflare's limits (CPU time, subrequests, body size), real SQLite behaviour, or a global the tab has and a Worker lacks. The guard's self-test on the uploaded version is what covers those.

### Knowing when a deploy is needed

The **hash** of a Worker declaration is the SHA-256 of its emitted module, library parts and metadata (methods, paths, bindings, secret names). It changes when the function or any cell it reaches changes. It does not change when prose, tests or tab-only cells change.

| where | what | written when |
|---|---|---|
| the tab | the hash of what the cell would emit now | recomputed when the cell or an ancestor changes |
| the saved notebook | \`workers: { NAME: { module, hash, methods, paths, secrets, resources } }\` in the bundle record, with the emitted module stored as a blob | the notebook is saved |
| the Worker | its answer to \`getInfo\` | set as a JSON binding at upload, so the hash need not contain itself |
| the guard | the hash it last deployed to each Worker | a deploy succeeds or is put back |

| state | means | action |
|---|---|---|
| in sync | tab, saved and Worker hashes are equal | none |
| changed, not saved | the tab differs from the saved record | Apply saves, then deploys |
| saved, not deployed | the saved record differs from the Worker | Apply deploys |
| not installed | no Worker of that name | Apply installs |
| skewed | the Worker differs from the guard's record | none automatic; the owner redeploys or looks |

Every Worker declaration registers under the plugin name \`workers\`. The Services list in \`cloud-brain\` reads that, asks each Worker \`getInfo\` through the kernel when it opens and after an Apply, and offers one Apply. \`serviceStatus(service)\` shows the same row beside a function. The guard deploys the stored module by hash; nothing is chased or compiled outside a tab.

### XRPC

XRPC is atproto's HTTP convention (\`atproto.com/specs/xrpc\`, read 2026-10-05): a method is \`/xrpc/<NSID>\`, a query is a GET, a procedure is a POST, an error is \`{ "error": "Name", "message": "…" }\`.

**Inside a Brain, Workers are addressed by method.** A Worker declaration states the methods it implements, each with a Lexicon. The core routes \`/xrpc/<NSID>\` to the Worker that declared it; each Worker holds one binding, to the core. A caller uses the \`xrpc\` platform cell, so a call is written the same in the tab and on a Worker. Replacing a service means implementing its methods.

**Between services, the Brain is an atproto service.** Measured on 2026-10-05 (Appendix B, E13 to E15).

| direction | how |
|---|---|
| the Brain is found | the kernel serves a DID document for \`did:web:<hostname>\` with a service entry \`#brain\`. A \`workers.dev\` hostname is enough |
| someone calls the Brain | their app sends the call to their PDS with \`atproto-proxy: did:web:<brain>#brain\`. The PDS forwards it with a JWT signed by the caller's atproto key: \`iss\` their DID, \`aud\` the Brain's DID, \`lxm\` the method, 60 s of life. The kernel checks it against the caller's DID document |
| the kernel decides | \`iss\` is the owner: caller is the owner. Another DID: only a method the owner granted it. \`secret.get\` and \`infra.*\` never accept a service JWT |
| the Brain calls out | a function calls \`xrpc.query(nsid, params, { service: "did:web:other#id" })\`. The kernel, holding the owner's atproto session, sends it to the owner's PDS with the proxy header |

Outside XRPC: pages, sign-in, the DID document, webhooks in a third party's shape, and WebSockets.

### The inbox

\`\`\`
inbox(id integer primary key, source, key unique, received_at, body, done_at)     SQLite, in the core
\`\`\`

| who | does |
|---|---|
| a recipe Worker | verifies the event, then \`inbox.append({ source, key, body })\`. \`key\` makes a retried delivery a no-op. It answers its caller without waiting for the tab |
| the guard | appends a deploy's result, a health failure, a rollback |
| the relay recipe | holds the lease and tells the tab holding it that the inbox changed |
| the tab | \`inbox.list\` after an id; passes each entry to the handlers registered under the plugin name \`inbox:SOURCE\`; \`inbox.done\`. On opening it takes everything not done |
| a recipe with no tab to deliver to | does what its channel allows: WhatsApp sends the link |

There is no outbox. An answer exists only while a tab is open, and the tab sends it by calling the recipe's method. An entry is delivered at least once, so a handler must tolerate seeing one twice.

### One notebook

A Brain is the Cloud Brain notebook. Its mains are \`cloud-brain\` (the UI), an instance of robocoop-5 (custom prompt, with its Cloud Brain instructions as a page in @tomlarkworthy/markdown-wiki), and one module per service: \`brain-kernel\`, \`brain-core\`, and a module per recipe. A recipe is a module with a \`cloudflare.Worker\` declaration, a \`setup\` prose cell, the functions a tab calls, its plugin registrations and tests. A recipe is distributed as its own notebook file. Dragging it into the Brain notebook adds its module to the mains; the module adds itself to the plugin registry and the Brain picks it up from there (Tom, 2026-10-05: "the WhatApp notebook is dragged into the notebook and adds itself to a plugin bus that the Brain picks up"). Saving keeps it.

