Kept as written on 2026-10-04, before the experiments and before the tab became the brain.

**The use case.** Tom, 2026-10-04: "I want cloud brain to be able to be connected to services like whats app so we can chat with it there … So we do need that [LLM credentials]. But I don't want divergence from the browser notebooks and the dataplane services … We do have robocoop-5 to be an agentic loop, so that should be reused somehow." An agent that can be reached from a chat service has to run with no tab open, so it runs in the dataplane, calls a model with a key from the secrets table, and should be able to change the Brain's own modules on request.

**What is already reusable.** \`@tomlarkworthy/robocoop-5-core\` (read 2026-10-04, canonical \`lopebooks/notebooks/@tomlarkworthy_robocoop-5.html\`) calls itself "the DOM-free brain of robocoop-5". \`createAgentSession({client, toolsProvider, systemPromptProvider, modelProvider, …})\` depends on \`AbortController\` and \`truncate\` only. \`createOpenRouterClient({apiKey, fetch, …})\` takes its key and its fetch as arguments. A dataplane module can import both, and the Worker export carries \`robocoop-5-core\` in the closure. What differs between a tab and the Brain is what is passed in: the client, the tools, the session store, the transport. Not read: whether the loop can be driven one step at a time from saved state.

**What does not carry over.** In a tab, robocoop-5 turns edited source into a live cell with \`realize\`, which needs dynamic import. Cloudflare's web-standards page (read 2026-10-04) lists \`eval()\`, \`new Function\`, \`WebAssembly.compile\`, \`compileStreaming\`, \`instantiate\` with a buffer and \`instantiateStreaming\` as restricted. The flag \`allow_eval_during_startup\` (default from compatibility date 2025-06-01) permits \`eval\` and \`new Function\` during startup only, where no I/O is possible, so only strings already in the upload can be evaluated. The page does not mention Durable Objects; the same rule is assumed.

Reading is not affected: inside a Worker the agent can list the runtime's variables, read their inputs, \`toString()\` their definitions and compute their values (Tom: "Its still in the runtime and can toString and do various things"). A userspace notebook in R2 is text to it until one of the options below runs it.

**Four ways to get new code running.** None is ruled out.

